1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,835
Bacteriostatic water.

2
00:00:01,835 --> 00:00:04,726
It sounds like it protects everything in the vial.

3
00:00:04,726 --> 00:00:06,002
It has one job.

4
00:00:06,002 --> 00:00:06,927
Here it is.

5
00:00:06,927 --> 00:00:12,734
Bacteriostatic water is sterile water with zero point nine percent benzyl alcohol.

6
00:00:12,734 --> 00:00:15,325
That benzyl alcohol is a preservative.

7
00:00:15,325 --> 00:00:20,325
It helps suppress bacterial growth, which is why it is labeled for multiple doses.

8
00:00:20,325 --> 00:00:21,471
Now compare.

9
00:00:21,471 --> 00:00:24,661
Sterile water for injection has no preservative.

10
00:00:24,661 --> 00:00:26,640
It is meant for a single use.

11
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,729
So bacteriostatic water works on the microbiology clock.

12
00:00:30,729 --> 00:00:34,270
What it does not do: it does not stop the chemistry clock.

13
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:36,705
It does not make a peptide last longer.

14
00:00:36,705 --> 00:00:40,169
And it is not complete protection against contamination either.

15
00:00:40,169 --> 00:00:41,119
One job.

16
00:00:41,119 --> 00:00:43,085
Suppress bacterial growth.

17
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:47,408
Everything else is still up to the label, the temperature, and the handling.

18
00:00:47,408 --> 00:00:48,684
Day four of ten.

19
00:00:48,684 --> 00:00:50,755
Follow so you don't miss day five.

